{-
   `TrivialCopy` and `TrivialSink` - the two classes nobody writes an instance of.

   They hold for a type exactly when they hold for every one of its members, which is the walk the
   ownership classifier already does for `Reclaim`, `Drop` and `Copy`. So there is no table entry to
   find and none may be written: selection answers them from the type's own shape, before it looks
   at anything a program declared.

   What they are *for* is the constraint. Design-Memory §2.1 fixes a generic body's behaviour by its
   own signature - an unconstrained parameter is treated as non-TrivialCopy inside the body whatever
   a caller substitutes - so a declared constraint is the only thing that can say otherwise, and a
   class is what a signature has to write one with.

   Both stages, because the point is that they agree. The shipping stage has answered these since
   `Program::coreClasses`, which finds the two declarations **by name**; the ownership stage reads
   `@builtin` and nothing else, so until `known.def` had rows for them `lib/Core`'s blanket
   `instance (TrivialCopy(a)) Copy(a)` could not discharge its own context and `Copy(I32)` failed
   with it - 27 of the 46 diagnostics that stage had left on the real library.

   The refusing half is `ownership/Reject.Structural.yana`, which names one stage because the two
   word the refusal differently.
-}

data Guard {id: Int}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->value: Guard) -> {} = {}

-- A scalar answers both, and answers them from the type rather than from an instance: nothing below
-- declares one, and nothing may.
fn (TrivialCopy(a)) copyable(x: a, n: Int) -> Int = n
fn (TrivialSink(a)) movable(x: a, n: Int) -> Int = n

-- A record of scalars is the fold: every member answers, so the record does.
data Point {x: Int, y: Int}

-- And one that does not. `Guard` has a `Drop`, so it is neither - which is what the record holding
-- one inherits, and what makes the constraint mean something rather than hold everywhere.
data Holder {guard: Guard}

fn main() -> Int:
    let scalar = copyable(7 :: Int, 1) + movable(9 :: Int, 2)
    let record = copyable(Point {x: 1, y: 2}, 4) + movable(Point {x: 3, y: 4}, 8)

    -- A `Holder` reaches neither, and the two functions above are the only callers - so what says
    -- so is `Reject.Structural.yana` rather than a line here.
    return scalar + record
